	Name :
================
MAC Flooding Attack

Why we did this
================
A switch uses a CAM (Content Addressable Memory) Table to store:

- MAC Address
- Associated Port

This allows the switch to forward frames efficiently.

Attackers discovered that a switch CAM table has limited memory.

By generating thousands of fake MAC addresses, an attacker can fill the CAM table until it becomes full.

When the CAM table is exhausted, the switch can no longer learn new MAC addresses.

The switch then starts flooding traffic out of all ports, similar to how a hub operates.

This allows attackers to capture traffic that was never intended for them.

This attack is known as a MAC Flooding Attack or CAM Table Overflow Attack.

What will happen
================
1. Attacker sends thousands of frames with fake source MAC addresses.
2. Switch continuously learns these fake MAC entries.
3. CAM table becomes full.
4. Switch cannot learn new MAC addresses.
5. Unknown traffic is flooded to multiple ports.
6. Attacker may capture traffic from other users.
7. Network performance may degrade.

Example

Normal Operation
----------------

PC1 ---> Switch ---> PC2

CAM Table

0011.1111.1111  Fa0/1
0022.2222.2222  Fa0/2

Result:
--------
Traffic sent only to destination port.


During Attack
-------------

Attacker sends:

AAAA.AAAA.0001
AAAA.AAAA.0002
AAAA.AAAA.0003
AAAA.AAAA.9999

CAM Table becomes full.

Result:
--------
Switch starts flooding unknown traffic.

Attacker can sniff traffic from other users.

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages (For Learning Purposes)
----------------------------------
1. Demonstrates CAM table limitations.
2. Helps understand Layer 2 attacks.
3. Useful for penetration testing labs.
4. Demonstrates why Port Security is important.
5. Validates switch security controls.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Can expose user traffic.
2. Enables packet sniffing.
3. Reduces switch performance.
4. Causes excessive flooding.
5. Increases security risk.
6. May impact business applications.
7. Can lead to information disclosure.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Administrators needed a way to prevent switches from learning unlimited MAC addresses.

Cisco introduced:

CAM Table Overflow Mitigation

using:

- Port Security
- Maximum MAC Limits
- Sticky MAC
- Violation Actions

These mechanisms stop excessive MAC learning and protect the CAM table.

